1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,036
Open your Google Sheets node, unpin the test data, and connect your real credential.

2
00:00:05,036 --> 00:00:07,769
Point Document and Sheet at your actual pipeline.

3
00:00:07,769 --> 00:00:12,064
The schedule you set in Section 5.2 means this now runs itself, every Monday,

4
00:00:12,064 --> 00:00:14,463
whether or not anyone thinks to trigger it.

5
00:00:14,463 --> 00:00:18,926
That is the whole point of building it this way: the twenty minutes it saves you

6
00:00:18,926 --> 00:00:21,492
happens whether or not you remember it exists.

7
00:00:21,492 --> 00:00:25,954
Expect the real sheet to be scruffier than your pinned rows, because real sheets

8
00:00:25,954 --> 00:00:30,361
always are: a stray empty row, a note typed under the table, a client name with

9
00:00:30,361 --> 00:00:33,485
trailing spaces, a second tab someone added last spring.

10
00:00:33,485 --> 00:00:37,948
None of that appeared in your test, which is exactly why the first live run gets

11
00:00:37,948 --> 00:00:41,239
read with the same answer-key discipline as the pinned one.

12
00:00:41,239 --> 00:00:44,698
Count your real pipeline by hand once, run the agent, compare.

13
00:00:44,698 --> 00:00:49,105
Where the live sheet trips it, the fix is usually to tidy the sheet rather than

14
00:00:49,105 --> 00:00:53,623
complicate the briefing: this agent earns its keep for years by reading one clean

15
00:00:53,623 --> 00:00:57,639
table, and clean is cheap to maintain once you know an agent is reading.

16
00:00:58,189 --> 00:01:02,885
That third point is an easy, boring mistake with a real cost: a report that lands at

17
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:07,525
2am your time because nobody checked the time zone setting is a report nobody reads

18
00:01:07,525 --> 00:01:09,481
before their actual Monday meeting.

19
00:01:09,481 --> 00:01:14,009
The first point has a principle inside it worth keeping for every scheduled thing

20
00:01:14,009 --> 00:01:17,922
you ever build: never let a schedule's first firing happen unobserved.

21
00:01:17,922 --> 00:01:22,394
The manual run is you watching the machine do the whole job once, end to end, on

22
00:01:22,394 --> 00:01:26,027
live data, while you are standing right there to read the result.

23
00:01:26,027 --> 00:01:28,934
After that, the clock takes over with your blessing.

24
00:01:28,934 --> 00:01:33,629
Skip it, and the first real run happens alone on Monday morning, and whatever it got

25
00:01:33,629 --> 00:01:37,990
wrong waits patiently in a draft until someone notices, which on a bad week is

26
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:38,493
Thursday.

27
00:01:38,493 --> 00:01:42,853
Watch the first one land, on purpose, with the sheet open beside it, and every

28
00:01:42,853 --> 00:01:46,263
Monday after that inherits the confidence of the one you saw.

29
00:01:46,813 --> 00:01:50,631
By now this step should feel routine, and that is exactly the point.

30
00:01:50,631 --> 00:01:54,169
Four different agents, one export habit, no new thing to learn.

31
00:01:54,169 --> 00:01:58,549
One wrinkle specific to this build: the export names the columns your briefing

32
00:01:58,549 --> 00:02:02,986
expects, so a colleague importing C03 onto their own sheet inherits your header

33
00:02:02,986 --> 00:02:04,558
contract without knowing it.

34
00:02:04,558 --> 00:02:08,995
When you hand this template to anyone, hand it with one sentence attached: your

35
00:02:08,995 --> 00:02:13,094
sheet needs these four headers, or the briefing needs your words instead.

36
00:02:13,094 --> 00:02:17,587
That sentence is the difference between a template that transplants and one that

37
00:02:17,587 --> 00:02:20,001
mysteriously underperforms in its new home.

38
00:02:20,551 --> 00:02:22,137
And after the three Mondays?

39
00:02:22,137 --> 00:02:26,669
The checking does not end, it thins, the way Section 1.2's ladder said it would.

40
00:02:26,669 --> 00:02:31,201
Priya's version at Review: every Monday she still reads the report before anyone

41
00:02:31,201 --> 00:02:35,846
else does, and one Monday in a few she re-counts one line of it against the sheet,

42
00:02:35,846 --> 00:02:36,809
chosen at random.

43
00:02:36,809 --> 00:02:40,945
Five minutes a month, permanently, in exchange for twenty minutes a week.

44
00:02:40,945 --> 00:02:45,703
That is the ratio this whole course is selling, and it only stays honest if the five

45
00:02:45,703 --> 00:02:47,063
minutes actually happen.

46
00:02:47,063 --> 00:02:51,595
C03 is also the template most worth re-aiming at other sheets in your life: same

47
00:02:51,595 --> 00:02:53,747
build, different columns, as 5.1 said.

48
00:02:53,747 --> 00:02:56,183
When you adapt it, the trial comes with it.

49
00:02:56,183 --> 00:02:57,826
New sheet, new three Mondays.

50
00:02:57,826 --> 00:02:59,469
Three agents down, one to go.

51
00:02:59,469 --> 00:03:04,114
Notice how each has stepped further from your hands: Agent 1 waited for the world,

52
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:06,947
Agent 2 waited for you, this one waits for nobody.

53
00:03:06,947 --> 00:03:11,478
The last agent takes the next step, towards writing words a client might one day

54
00:03:11,478 --> 00:03:15,954
read, and that is exactly why it is the most carefully constrained of the four.

55
00:03:15,954 --> 00:03:17,030
Section 6 explains.
